  "worked_solution": "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(10v)=10Av=10(5v)=5(10v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(10v)=5(10v),$ and $10v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $10v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =6v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=6v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =6.$\n\n- Statement III is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=4v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\".",
